WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.300
- Today on Drew.

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:05.130
Get the story behind the scenes.

00:00:06.127 --> 00:00:08.098
- Tell her to unmute.

00:00:08.098 --> 00:00:09.470
- Can you hear us?

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:11.303
- Of season one surprises.

00:00:12.279 --> 00:00:15.291
The gag was all David's idea.

00:00:15.291 --> 00:00:16.490
And

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:18.889
we reveal our very first celebrity guest

00:00:18.889 --> 00:00:22.000
and she is a very good friend.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.776
It all starts now.

00:00:23.776 --> 00:00:26.359
(upbeat music)

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:30.750
- [Announcer] Please
welcome Drew Barrymore!

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.970
- Hi everybody, welcome!

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.440
The season two premiere
of the Drew Barrymore show

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.730
is less than a week away.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:40.680
September 13th, check your local listings

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:42.950
or go to the drewbarrymoreshow.com.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.048
Go to Show Finder, type in your zip code

00:00:45.048 --> 00:00:47.410
(fingers snap) and boom
you'll find out right away.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:49.110
I couldn't be more excited.

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:52.960
We're kicking things off
with a huge LA premiere.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.670
I'm going back to the town that made me

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:58.140
for a whole week of Hollywood shows

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.570
because there's no place like home.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:05.500
And then I'm returning to
my new home here in New York

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:09.080
for another week of New
York city Premier shows

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.490
the week of September 20th.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:15.000
It's 10 days of celebrating our roots

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.740
and we'll be highlighting home
towns all across the country.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:20.520
Maybe even yours?

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.310
Go ahead back to the website,

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:27.140
drewbarrymoreshow.com and let
us know why your home town

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.160
is special, to you.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.090
But before we get to all of that,

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:34.200
we've got this great round
up of our favorite moments

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.150
from season one, with some bonus content.

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:40.340
A little behind the
scenes commentary from me

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:43.080
on what really went down.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:44.680
So let's kick it off,

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:47.960
with a moment that will
stay with me forever.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:52.960
My producers and the whole
team planned a birthday show

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:56.890
for me and I wasn't in on one detail.

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:59.500
I asked, "Please don't tell me anything.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.160
I don't want to know.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:03.110
I don't think you're gonna do anything

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:05.810
but whatever you do, I'll
just, you know, not know."

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.830
And then all of a sudden
there were surprises

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:13.830
and gosh, you wouldn't
even believe who showed up.

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:18.870
I didn't even know if
there would be anyone here,

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.193
but guess who came?

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.530
David Letterman.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:27.530
Someone who is very much
inextricably linked in my history

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:31.610
and he came in-studio,
in-person during the pandemic.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:34.220
So it meant all the more.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.970
And he wished me happy birthday

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.920
and birthdays were kind of
a thing between him and I.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:40.483
Anyway, uh, take a look.

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:42.263
- I'm gonna give you some clues.

00:02:42.263 --> 00:02:43.180
Okay?
- Okay.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:44.300
- Let's see how you would do.

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:47.660
He would be on your top 10 list of guests,

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:49.313
I think you'd want at any party.

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:55.300
You once surprised him on his birthday.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:56.380
- Oh my God.

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:58.040
- And his stupid human trick
- David.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:02.450
- is being one, the greatest
television hosts of all time.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:04.230
Do you know who this might be?

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:05.850
- I know exactly who it is.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.000
- Who is it?
- David Letterman.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.622
- Let us welcome David Letterman, hi Dave!

00:03:11.622 --> 00:03:13.590
(audience applauding)

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:14.660
- Hi everybody!

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:16.620
Hi Drew!

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:19.040
Happy birthday!

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.460
Savannah, hello, how are you?

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:23.140
- Hi, she's dying, she can't believe it!

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:24.732
- Oh my gosh!

00:03:24.732 --> 00:03:25.820
- Can you hear me?
- I'm having a hard time

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:28.298
catching my breath, I can hear you!

00:03:28.298 --> 00:03:29.947
Dave!

00:03:29.947 --> 00:03:31.571
- I can't, I can't hear her.

00:03:31.571 --> 00:03:34.272
- Oh, Dave--
- Is there a filter?

00:03:34.272 --> 00:03:35.687
- Wait.
- Do you have a filter on?

00:03:35.687 --> 00:03:39.967
I can't, tell her to unmute!
Could you unmute? (laughter)

00:03:39.967 --> 00:03:41.360
- Are you zooming, Dave?

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:43.495
What's like, are you good at technology--

00:03:43.495 --> 00:03:48.495
- I'm not a cat, I can't,
I'm sorry I can't hear her.

00:03:48.603 --> 00:03:50.110
- Well you look like a fine cat.

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:51.490
- Can you hear me?
- Okay, it's the thought

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:53.500
that counts, Dave!
- Yes, we can hear you!

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:55.993
- We hear you but can you hear us?

00:03:55.993 --> 00:03:58.080
- [Dave] Forget it, forget it.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:03:59.000
Forget that.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:01.820
- Can you believe, I mean that's
so sweet of him to do that.

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:03.090
- I know!
- When was the last time

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:04.480
you actually saw Dave?
- I feel the same

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:05.730
way he does about Zoom.

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:08.519
Especially when my daughters crash--

00:04:08.519 --> 00:04:12.828
(upbeat music)
(crowd cheering)

00:04:12.828 --> 00:04:14.078
- Hi everybody.

00:04:17.173 --> 00:04:19.619
How are you?
Hi Savannah!

00:04:19.619 --> 00:04:21.619
- Hi Dave!
- How are you?

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:23.857
- Hi!

00:04:23.857 --> 00:04:26.010
- You look wonderful!

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:27.610
Nice to see you, happy birthday!

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:32.660
What do you think?

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:34.770
Here, sit down.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:36.413
Oh my!

00:04:36.413 --> 00:04:37.829
- (sobbing) I'm--
(Dave laughing)

00:04:37.829 --> 00:04:39.053
- We're going to need the tissues.

00:04:39.053 --> 00:04:41.760
- I'm so grateful that you came here.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:42.880
- Let me tell you something.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.830
You know that a little trick
with the computer and stuff?

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:46.858
That Zoomer thing?
That was my idea!

00:04:46.858 --> 00:04:49.830
- That was good, you still got it!

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:52.428
- Oh thank you!
- It was really effective,

00:04:52.428 --> 00:04:56.557
I really thought, I was like,
"Okay, Dave, I get it too!"

00:04:57.650 --> 00:04:59.404
- You're actually crying!

00:04:59.404 --> 00:05:03.093
Oh that breaks my heart, God bless you.

00:05:03.093 --> 00:05:07.027
- I'm so grateful that
you are showing up for me.

00:05:07.027 --> 00:05:10.600
- I can't thank you enough for everything

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:13.930
and the friendship that
you demonstrated for us

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:17.450
when we were on the show and
it meant a great deal then,

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:19.410
and this means a great deal as well.

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:22.768
Happy birthday, and thank you
for, or did I invite myself?

00:05:22.768 --> 00:05:23.601
- No!

00:05:23.601 --> 00:05:24.910
- I don't know how this happened.

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:26.750
- You're this, you're
kind of the guest of honor

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:27.720
next to Drew, but wait--

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:29.820
- The poor dear is actually crying.

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:31.260
- She's crying, she's shocked.

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:33.094
I told her, she really
thought she might know

00:05:33.094 --> 00:05:33.927
who was coming--

00:05:33.927 --> 00:05:35.061
- I love you, I really do!
- Well I love you as well!

00:05:35.061 --> 00:05:36.990
- Okay, wait, hold on, this is so weird.

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.850
It's Drew's birthday,
I'm Savannah Guthrie.

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:41.590
I'm here to celebrate Drew.

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:44.430
Oh, just David Letterman,
swinging by to shock Drew.

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:47.200
I don't think surprise is
the word, I think it's shock.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.710
- And I kept saying she has to know.

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:53.820
This woman is a seasoned
show business veteran

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:56.770
in front of the camera, behind
the camera in her office,

00:05:56.770 --> 00:06:00.490
anywhere want to put her, she
has to know this is going on.

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:02.680
And then you come out
here and you're crying

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:05.140
and that was so touching and
very sweet of you, thank you.

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:07.385
- I'm just so honored
that you show up for me.

00:06:07.385 --> 00:06:12.120
I mean it, because I feel
even weird saying "Up for me,"

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:14.497
I'm just so happy to see
you as an old friend.

00:06:14.497 --> 00:06:16.050
- It's been a long time.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:17.770
I know it's not my show to produce,

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:21.190
but how great would this be
if it was really a surprise

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:23.120
for Savannah Guthrie?

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:24.521
Ladies and gentleman!
- If I just got up

00:06:24.521 --> 00:06:29.521
on this table and you know,
in fact, I think we should

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:34.030
roll a certain clip.
- Get off the furniture!

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:35.995
- I mean, what is going on?

00:06:35.995 --> 00:06:37.542
- Would you like me to do a dance for you?

00:06:37.542 --> 00:06:40.210
(crowd cheering)

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:41.621
- What?
- Hey Paul!

00:06:41.621 --> 00:06:44.590
Hi Paul, Paul gimme some blues!

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:45.590
- Oh my god.

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:05.180
- Oh, oh my goodness.
- I... that happened.

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:08.539
Dave, do you remember that?
Anything about that?

00:07:08.539 --> 00:07:13.360
- I'll tell you, honestly,
I used to watch old movies.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:15.690
Blackboard Jungle, Glenn Ford,

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:18.999
and what happens is the
juvenile delinquent teenagers

00:07:18.999 --> 00:07:22.710
take over the classroom and
they're up on Glenn Ford's desk

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:23.850
and they're going to beat him up.

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:28.850
And so Drew is there, I love
the "Hi Paul." (girl laughing)

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:33.157
And then she climbs up on the
desk and I thought to myself,

00:07:33.157 --> 00:07:36.470
"Oh no, I've lost control
of the class!" (laughter)

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:38.457
And then my next thought was,

00:07:38.457 --> 00:07:41.150
"I think I'm probably
going to get kicked."

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:42.810
And then everything changed.

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:44.200
You know, like in The Wizard of Oz,

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:46.760
when it goes from black
and white to color,

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:48.205
that's what it was like.

00:07:48.205 --> 00:07:52.270
- I had no plan of doing that,

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:57.020
it was one of those moments
in my life where I felt like

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:01.180
a magnet was pulling me
and it was all very playful

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:04.330
and well-intended, and I just had no idea

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:05.830
where it was going.

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:08.920
And I almost was like,
"Should I be embarrassed?

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:11.780
Did I do something bad?
Am I in trouble?"

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:15.340
And I knew in that
moment that with one turn

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:18.380
you could have gone, "Why did you do that?

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:20.220
What's wrong with you?"

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:25.220
And instead you had that smile
and you said to everyone,

00:08:26.237 --> 00:08:27.987
"This moment is okay."

00:08:28.971 --> 00:08:31.221
(laughter)

00:08:32.181 --> 00:08:35.020
- Oh my God, oh my God!
- That was the moment.

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:38.660
I hope that I can be half
the man Dave is right now

00:08:38.660 --> 00:08:41.840
and just let something
innocent and spontaneous

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:43.580
be innocent and spontaneous.

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:47.969
- Well, I recognized it
immediately is being unique.

00:08:47.969 --> 00:08:52.969
It had not happened prior
to me has not happened

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:56.697
since to me, and later I was thinking,

00:08:56.697 --> 00:09:00.363
"Oh geez, now what if she goes
and does this on Leno show,

00:09:01.349 --> 00:09:04.230
then I'll really be upset."

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:09.230
But it was kind of a connection
for us throughout our,

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:10.880
the rest of our existence here.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:13.960
- You compelled me in
a moment to take a risk

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:17.450
and you allowed it to be
paid off with kindness

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:20.490
and reciprocity and goodness and humor,

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:22.130
which is my ultimate.

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:24.340
Honestly, I think that's
the greatest award

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:26.520
and reward that people can
give each other in life.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.230
- Oh, later we were, of course, married--

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:30.520
- Yes, we went to a
church and got married.

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:33.200
Look at me, I'm like,
I think you're so cute.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:35.550
I did, I've had a crush on you forever so.

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:37.430
- We were happy for
another 20 minutes there.

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:39.490
- That's right, like a normal marriage.

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:41.252
- Drew was on your show 20 times.

00:09:41.252 --> 00:09:42.777
- 20 times, that's remarkable.
- That's got to be one of your

00:09:42.777 --> 00:09:44.680
best guests.
- Well again, yes,

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:49.190
absolutely and this is
why all credit to you,

00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:51.465
because in the world of show business,

00:09:51.465 --> 00:09:53.910
you have two things, you have
show business friendships,

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:55.906
which are not really friendships.

00:09:55.906 --> 00:09:57.077
And then you have actual friendships,

00:09:57.077 --> 00:10:01.286
and I considered our relationship
to be a true friendship.

00:10:01.286 --> 00:10:04.084
(applause)
Thank you.

00:10:04.084 --> 00:10:08.150
- I was not there for shock value.

00:10:08.150 --> 00:10:10.800
I was there to share a moment with you

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:13.500
that took on a little
bit of a life of its own,

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:16.930
but my heart was so
well-intended towards you.

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:19.930
- Well, now let's talk
about the circumstance now.

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:22.410
Are you enjoying this part of your life?

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:25.653
- Yeah, as hard and challenging as it is,

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:30.820
I just feel like when I
wake up in the morning,

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:34.420
I wake up to Savannah and Gale.

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:38.130
I go to sleep to people
like you, and you know,

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:40.803
I think of a Jimmy
Fallon, who's like family,

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:46.670
and you guys are who jumpstart
and close out our days.

00:10:47.890 --> 00:10:50.790
And that's why I felt so
privileged to step into a role

00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:54.350
that has been so important
to me as a citizen

00:10:54.350 --> 00:10:56.540
and a human being walking around.

00:10:56.540 --> 00:10:58.960
You are literally telling people

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:01.460
it's okay to start their day,
here's how it's going to go,

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:03.110
here's some optimism, here's some news,

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:04.070
here's some information.

00:11:04.070 --> 00:11:06.720
And you would say, "You
got through the day,

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:09.490
I'm going to put you to
bed, congratulations.

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:10.760
Now you're allowed to laugh."

00:11:10.760 --> 00:11:12.520
- This is a great way
for you to look at it

00:11:12.520 --> 00:11:16.096
because it's true, the
landscape in the early morning,

00:11:16.096 --> 00:11:19.306
middle of the day, or whenever we see you,

00:11:19.306 --> 00:11:22.030
several other people
have helped along the way

00:11:22.030 --> 00:11:25.110
have been very beneficial,
Oprah was fantastic.

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:26.797
- So, Dave, do you like being a guest?

00:11:26.797 --> 00:11:28.860
- No, no, I'm very uncomfortable about it.

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:31.210
Although, I'm very pleased
about the Zoomer thing.

00:11:31.210 --> 00:11:33.440
And I mentioned that was my idea.

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:35.263
- It was good.
- It worked.

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:37.590
- No, because I have nothing,

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:38.640
let me tell you something.

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:41.482
Do I, can I talk, a lot of
places don't want me to talk.

00:11:41.482 --> 00:11:43.380
- (giggles) Not here.

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:45.360
- It's interesting about life.

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:49.080
You learn things over
that you already learned,

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:53.360
and then you forget because
you're drawn toward the mundane.

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:58.000
So you can't be sucked into
the reality, the drama,

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:01.030
and the ugliness that
can be provided by life.

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:03.160
So you're drawn toward the mundane

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:05.240
and you learn these
lessons again and again.

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:09.410
And because of the pandemic,
you know people who have had it

00:12:09.410 --> 00:12:12.666
and perhaps died, I know
people who have died,

00:12:12.666 --> 00:12:14.330
same with all of us.

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:16.700
We're looking at a half a million people.

00:12:16.700 --> 00:12:20.170
I don't know how many wars
that is, but a half a million,

00:12:20.170 --> 00:12:23.020
stunning that we're living with every day.

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:27.460
So I think humanity is temporary.

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:30.800
And we all know that and
because it's temporary,

