00:20:02.531 --> 00:20:05.300
- Food! I love eating it!

00:20:05.300 --> 00:20:07.800
Talking about it, watching it on TV.

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:11.610
Here is delicious kitchen inspiration.

00:20:11.610 --> 00:20:13.930
- Hi Drew! Thanks for
having me back on the show.

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:17.070
Today, I'm going to show
you how to tackle steak.

00:20:17.070 --> 00:20:19.410
- It's always better to
season your steak with salt

00:20:19.410 --> 00:20:20.243
ahead of time.

00:20:20.243 --> 00:20:22.320
A few hours is fine,
but overnight is ideal.

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:24.520
This will allow the salt
penetrate all the way through

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:25.670
to the center of the steak.

00:20:25.670 --> 00:20:27.680
Set this on a wire rack and
place it in your fridge.

00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:28.870
Uncover for airflow.

00:20:28.870 --> 00:20:30.550
If you got an insta pot,
they all come with this

00:20:30.550 --> 00:20:31.383
little rack.

00:20:31.383 --> 00:20:32.216
How do you know when
your pan is hot enough?

00:20:32.216 --> 00:20:34.210
With this hack, you'll
forever get a good sear

00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:35.540
on your steak, every single time.

00:20:35.540 --> 00:20:37.300
Drop a teaspoon of water into your pan

00:20:37.300 --> 00:20:39.630
and when it looks like
T-1000 out of Terminator,

00:20:39.630 --> 00:20:41.060
it means your pan is ready.

00:20:41.060 --> 00:20:42.700
This is called the Leidenfrost effect,

00:20:42.700 --> 00:20:44.560
and if your water sticks and bubbles out,

00:20:44.560 --> 00:20:46.790
it means your pan is too
cold and it's not ready.

00:20:46.790 --> 00:20:49.560
For New York strips, I
like to add zero oil.

00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:51.760
Fat side down first, render out that fat,

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:54.890
and then seer both sides for
about two minutes per side.

00:20:54.890 --> 00:20:57.270
When you're ready to butter
bast, turn down the heat to low

00:20:57.270 --> 00:20:59.240
because butter burns at high temperatures,

00:20:59.240 --> 00:21:01.700
which can leave burnt
bitter taste onto the steak

00:21:01.700 --> 00:21:04.840
and butter absolutely loves
aromatics like Thyme and garlic

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:06.950
because they're good buddies
and they infuse really,

00:21:06.950 --> 00:21:08.000
really well together.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:09.840
A digital thermometer is a must.

00:21:09.840 --> 00:21:12.610
Always remove the steak 10
degrees lower than your desired

00:21:12.610 --> 00:21:14.460
doneness, because the
steak will continue to cook

00:21:14.460 --> 00:21:15.460
even when it's out of the pan.

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:16.570
Cover and let it rest.

00:21:16.570 --> 00:21:18.590
Resting allows the
muscle proteins to relax

00:21:18.590 --> 00:21:21.420
so it can retain more of
it's own juices and flavor.

00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:24.290
I like to make a quick sauce
using horseradish, a mustard,

00:21:24.290 --> 00:21:25.230
salt, and pepper.

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:26.880
I always cut my steaks at a bias

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:30.070
because it always makes
for a better meat fan.

00:21:30.070 --> 00:21:32.670
And finally, don't sleep
on the resting juices.

00:21:32.670 --> 00:21:33.920
This is where the magic is at.

00:21:33.920 --> 00:21:34.940
Now, watch this move.

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:38.353
Whew, now you tell me if
this will taste good or not.

00:21:41.854 --> 00:21:43.430
Mm!

00:21:43.430 --> 00:21:45.220
And that's how you cook a steak!

00:21:45.220 --> 00:21:49.503
- Wow, I'm in a delicious food
coma just for watching that!

00:22:00.460 --> 00:22:01.610
Welcome back.

00:22:01.610 --> 00:22:04.320
With everything going on
in the world these days,

00:22:04.320 --> 00:22:08.220
we can't lose sight of the
fact that there are real heroes

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:10.520
among us, that's right.

00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:13.890
People who do good
things, and on this show,

00:22:13.890 --> 00:22:17.910
we like to shine a light on
those we call Drew Gooders.

00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:21.130
And one of my favorite was
from season one, 18 year old,

00:22:21.130 --> 00:22:24.070
Justin Gavin, from Brooklyn, New York.

00:22:24.070 --> 00:22:27.080
We formed a friendship and we
have a payoff for this one.

00:22:27.080 --> 00:22:27.933
Take a look.

00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:32.780
He saw a burning car on
the street with a mother

00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:37.440
and her three children inside
and without hesitation,

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:41.150
he risked his own life to
pull each one of them out

00:22:41.150 --> 00:22:44.163
of that burning car into safety.

00:22:45.343 --> 00:22:48.080
(applause)

00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:52.593
Please welcome right here
in the studio, Justin Gavin.

00:22:55.851 --> 00:22:57.430
- Hi!

00:22:57.430 --> 00:23:01.707
- I am really star struck
to meet her real life hero.

00:23:01.707 --> 00:23:05.490
- I mean, I wouldn't call
myself a hero, I just thought,

00:23:05.490 --> 00:23:07.220
I just thought I just did what was right.

00:23:07.220 --> 00:23:09.290
So I'm not going to call myself hero.

00:23:09.290 --> 00:23:13.410
- But may I ask when you see that,

00:23:13.410 --> 00:23:16.240
what goes on inside of your head and body?

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:18.800
Is it a trajectory that just happens?

00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:22.837
Are you thinking, what does it feel like?

00:23:22.837 --> 00:23:27.240
- To me, I feel like at that
moment it was no thinking.

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:28.800
Like I just thought what was right,

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:30.020
I knew if I was in that situation,

00:23:30.020 --> 00:23:32.980
I would definitely want someone
to do exactly what I did,

00:23:32.980 --> 00:23:34.403
but not all people was like that,

00:23:34.403 --> 00:23:36.800
but I'm just glad I was at the right place

00:23:36.800 --> 00:23:38.460
at the right time.

00:23:38.460 --> 00:23:41.860
- You really were, so what were you,

00:23:41.860 --> 00:23:45.533
you were just walking down
the street on your merry way?

00:23:46.820 --> 00:23:49.240
- Yeah, I was, I was running an errand,

00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:51.010
and I just saw a bunch of cars

00:23:51.010 --> 00:23:53.060
and they were just honking their horns

00:23:53.060 --> 00:23:55.360
and they were yelling
out, "The car is on fire,

00:23:55.360 --> 00:23:57.010
The cars are fire!" and I was clueless.

00:23:57.010 --> 00:23:58.350
I didn't know what was happening.

00:23:58.350 --> 00:24:02.140
So I actually did see
the car after a while

00:24:02.140 --> 00:24:03.653
and I'm like, "Oh, get out
the car, get out the car.

00:24:03.653 --> 00:24:07.090
The car is on fire." and nobody
was running towards the car,

00:24:07.090 --> 00:24:08.850
was trying to do anything to help out,

00:24:08.850 --> 00:24:10.270
so I just ran towards the car.

00:24:10.270 --> 00:24:13.570
- And do you reach in at that
point, do you open doors?

00:24:13.570 --> 00:24:15.104
Are, what are you doing?

00:24:15.104 --> 00:24:17.410
- I told the oldest,
which is nine years old

00:24:17.410 --> 00:24:20.100
to like unbuckle her seatbelt
and her sibling seatbelt.

00:24:20.100 --> 00:24:24.428
It was nine, four, and
one, I believe that in the,

00:24:24.428 --> 00:24:26.820
it was scary for me cause I didn't know

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:27.740
what was going to happen.

00:24:27.740 --> 00:24:29.950
I thought at that time I was
actually going to lose my life

00:24:29.950 --> 00:24:31.230
and they were gonna lose their life.

00:24:31.230 --> 00:24:34.010
But if I did lose my life
I would lose my life trying

00:24:34.010 --> 00:24:37.400
to save somebody else
so it was... (applause)

